GPT-6. Checked 2026-10-29 05:58 UTC; read-only review, no files modified.

1. HIGH — Column (b1) is not generally unbiased. bar_top3.py:111–112 estimates beta from the same observations used in the corrected mean. Consequently, E[beta_hat(g_bar−Eg)] need not vanish. Exact box probabilities do not remove this dependence. Use an independent pilot or cross-fitting, including its cost, before claiming unbiasedness. This affects results/top3_*.json:b1; the current MSEs remain measurements of the implemented, potentially biased estimator.

2. HIGH — The “break even” conclusion changes Monte Carlo baselines. draft5.md:1010–1011 defines analytic Monte Carlo MSE, but lines 1034–1037 obtain 1.08x/1.16x by scaling empirical estim_T16.json and estim_W64s001ck.json:est.mc MSEs. Applying the stated formula to top3_*.json:b1/b2 gives best equal-FLOP gains of 0.929x on T16 and 1.284x on W64; W64 b1 peaks at 1.171x. Thus T16’s point-estimate win disappears. State both baselines explicitly, propagate uncertainty, and weaken E7.4’s “hit.” The summary and progress log’s unqualified “breaks even” also need qualification.

3. MEDIUM — “Integrated exactly” misdescribes the evidence. draft5.md:77–78, 155, 934–936 and 993 quote results/top3conv_*.json:mc_integral, which top3_conv.py:14–16 computes by Monte Carlo averaging. These are estimates of the integral, with standard errors, not exact evaluations. The underlying order-statistic transformation and probability weights are mathematically correct; implementation uses interpolated CDFs and numerical bivariate quadrature. Checked inverse-CDF boundary discrepancies were approximately 10^-9, practically negligible. Distinguish an exact distributional construction from exact integration.

4. MEDIUM — The causal attribution exceeds the experiment. draft5.md:937–938 attributes “most” earlier bias to closure in algorithmic variables, but TOP3 also changes the conditional residual model; no controlled comparison isolates closure. Lines 1027–1030 similarly extrapolate base-model convergence diagnostics to the entire perturbed family. top3conv_*.json contains only base-model integrals. Cancellation is well supported on the bases, but its magnitude across all 16 copies is unmeasured. Retain the observed improvement without that causal decomposition.

5. MEDIUM — K7’s budget interpretation is unstated. PREREG.md:292 does not specify “finest,” unlike E7.1. Both models exceed 50% relative error at each of the first three budgets: top3_*.json:a[2].est[0] is −50.77% on T16 and −52.19% on W64. “Did not fire” at draft5.md:1054 therefore needs an explicit finest-budget interpretation. Y1–Y5 and E7.1–E7.3 otherwise have the reported numerical outcomes, subject to their stated budget qualifications. Keep added-budget results exploratory; the 64x minimum comes from post-smoke (6,10).

6. MEDIUM — Column-(b2) costs are approximate beyond the stated accounting. bar_top3.py:120 rounds allocations and floors each at two, so actual sample count is sum(Nh), while lines 134–135 charge N. Stratum construction and categorical-distribution preparation at lines 115–123 are also omitted. The stratified estimator is unbiased under ideal exact box sampling because allocations depend only on predictions; unequal rounded counts do not bias it. Charge actual counts and preparation before interpreting small FLOP advantages.

7. LOW — Numerical/protocol corrections: draft5.md:997 should give 4.17%, not 4.18% (top3conv_T16.json:quad[2].p); line 1050’s −13% rounds to −14%; line 1030’s +0.55 points rounds to +0.56. sysid.py:90–94 averages share_A/share_B instead of reporting Amendment 7’s registered share_B. This valid held-out variant leaves prediction verdicts unchanged but should be disclosed.
